## Introduction
The utilization of clean and renewable energy has been an important issue of modern human life in the $21^{\mathrm{st}}$ century [1-3]. It becomes more and more significant to explore efficient conversion from the intermittent renewable resources into usable electricity and the realization of electricity storage. Electrochemical energy conversion and storage technologies hold great promise in practical applications [4-6], but face enormous challenges towards highly efficient electrode materials. Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) have attracted considerable attentions among various candidates due to their unique advantages such as controllable pore sizes and high specific surface area [7,8]. MOFs have been generally regarded as coordination networks with potential porosity [9]. The arrangement of the moieties within the network is extremely regular, and thus MOFs are seen to be crystalline [10] with both short-range order and long-range order for a long time [11,12]. Since 2003, a growing family of amorphous MOFs (aMOFs) without long-range order gradually started to share the attentions with crystalline MOF (cMOF) as shown in Fig. 1.
aMOFs serve as a subset of amorphous coordination polymers that emerge prospective voids and two or three dimensions frame structures [10,13]. For simplicity, aMOFs consist of the framework structure of inorganic nodes (metal ions or clusters)
linked with organic ligands, similar to the building blocks of cMOFs, but without long-range order [14]. The structures of first aMOF were not clear enough in 2003[15]. Related characterizations look indistinguishable from other materials such as amorphous carbon/oxides or organic polymers/metal ions. Determining the network at the microscopic scale is complex, with only indirect structural information available from scattering or diffraction experiments like X-ray or neutron. The first wellcharacterized aMOF appeared with developing characterization methodologies until 2010 [16]. Taking a typical amorphous zeolitic imidazolate frameworks (aZIFs) as an example, neutron and X-ray total scattering together with reverse Monte Carlo refinement simulation were used to confirm the atomistic model. aZIF-4 presents an isotropic glasslike phase with continuously random network similar to that of $\mathrm{aSiO}_{2}$ This work provides a good reference point for future aMOF works. Atomistic insights into aMOF require multi-scale structural characterization technologies combined with simulation models to avoid misusing the concept of amorphous MOF [17]. These structural understandings further endow accurate synthesis. Followed the first successful amorphization strategy, aMOFs were all realized via top-down post-processing of crystalline frameworks at the initial stage. For example, the first aMOF was discovered via breaking the crystalline long-range periodicity of cMOF based on removed coordinated solvent and rapid volume contraction. Then in 201l, electrical discharge was employed to achieve the partially amorphous MOF-5 via the destruction of carboxylate groups and the collapse of pores [l8]. Four years later, the rapid amorphization of $Z\mathrm{r}$ -based MOFs (UiO-66,MIL-140）was found during ball-milling [19]. In the same year, a bottom-up approach accomplished amorphous MOF  spheres via the  ligand competition [20]. Since then, versatile accessibility of aMOFs has stepped forward to the discovery of new aMOFs.
The development of synthesis provides material basis and unique structural models for practical applications. Benefits from intrinsic superiorities over crystalline states have drawn the attention in electrochemical energy conversion and storage fields. First, aMOFs retain the basic skeletons and connectivity with ligands. Structural features over long range possess the isotropy without grain boundaries, resulting in fast ion flux and uniform distribution. Second, distinct short-range characteristics provide diverse pore confined environment and abundant active sites, and thus benefit for mass transport and charge transfer during electrochemical reactions. All these merits of aMOF contribute to their flourishing in  the electrochemical  energy  fields. Corresponding reports first appeared in 20l6 [21], where Mnbased aMOFs were employed as the active coating to strengthen the stability of Li-ion batteries (LIBs). aMOFs in electrochemical energy conversion and storage fields are explored yet in their infancy, but have already shown enormous potentials compared with their crystalline counterparts after nearly a decade of development [22-25]. Amorphous Ni-p-phenylenediamine (aNipPD)was first proposed for the supercapacitor with high power density in 2019. After two years, aMIL-88B and aZIF-4 have exhibited high capabilities in Li-S battery and solid battery to pursue boosted energy density and satisfied safety. Turning the eyes to electrochemical energy conversion fields, we found that continuous studies also confirmed aMOFs' prospect since the first report of $\mathrm{aFe}_{x}\mathrm{Ni}_{y}$ -BDC for water oxidation. Overall, the past four years have been witnessed an astounding increase in publications regarding aMOFs and their electrochemical applications.
Through intensive efforts over 19 years, aMOF has been wellknown in MOF chemistry. Several breakthroughs have been achieved, but to the best of our knowledge, comprehensive reviews of aMOF towards electrochemical energy conversion and storage remain woefully underrepresented. It is desirable to provide an overview with integrated strategies in the broader context of various aMOFs. Herein,we summarize the roadmap of aMOFs from the views of their history, opportunity, application, bottleneck, andperspective.In-depth structure-activity dependence with aMOF chemistry is highlighted, taking the typical electrochemical energy conversion like water oxidation and energy storage, including supercapacitor and battery, as examples. The combination of disordered nature at multiple ranges, alongside the dynamic structural changes, is promising to reinforce cognition of aMOF domains with MOF versatility, shedding light on the design for efficient electrode materials via amorphization. The emerging aMOFs are worth keeping exploring in the near future.
## 2 Opportunities
aMOFs stand out as an emerging whereas already extensive area towards defined functionality. The realization of aMOFs was promoted by the developing synthetic strategies for large-scale applications. Tracing the previous progress, the realization strategies in Fig.2 can be overviewed by the formation sequence of amorphous states into two major kinds, top-down and bottom-up approaches. Top-down approaches as the dominant synthetic methods are achieved via introducing disorder into cMOFs during amorphization post-processing. A variety of external driving forces have been applied to break the long-range periodicity and induce the structural collapse of cMOFs successfully, such as physical stress (static and hydrostatic pressures [26,27],mechanical stress [19] like ball milling, and grinding, etc.), chemical treatment (solvent removel [28], atmospheric water [29],ligand competition [24], and nanoparticle loading [30],etc.), heating [31], and radiation [32]. In addition, a small but growing number of aMOFs appeared through bottom-up synthesis. Corresponding aMOFs are always porous with little structural collapse, which differs from the atomistic aMOF models from top-down approaches. Modulating diverse reaction parameters offers more possibilities for targeted design. But such bottom-up method is yet in an early stage. The design principles need to be explored in depth. Tracking previous progress, only $\sim110$ types of aMOFs were reported [14],which nonetheless is not as abundant as other classes of materials. It is envisaged that the fusion of soft matter sciences with porous materials would result in the discovery and creation of numerous novel materials and devices.
Reliable structural models are significant to develop the structural-performance dependences and further rational design of aMOFs for specific applications. In contrast to these potential properties, aMOFs are more difficult to define than their crystalline counterparts [l7]. But intensive efforts have been devoted to advanced charactering for local and extended structures by multidisciplinary characterizations. Understanding these characterization techniques can help in identifying the shortand long-range characteristics of aMOFs. Bragg diffraction is always used to illuminate the long-range order of materials. aMOFs are lack of long-range order, so it is impossible to get any Bragg diffraction of aMOF and further determine the positions of individual atom [13]. But strong scattering owing to the disordered  feature provides valuable  information  about atom-atom interactions, and thus contributing to the insights into short-range characteristics. The radial distribution function (RDF) or pair distribution function (PDF) is an efficient local structure probe of atom-atom distances. Corresponding results can realize the explore of the short-range perturbations and the degree of local and intermediate order within amorphous solids under the premise of fixed composition and density of the material to be analyzed. Indeed, it is widely recognized that determining the structural characteristics at short range is complex, with only indirect information from total scattering or diffraction measurements like X-ray, neutron, or electron beam[33,34]. The coordination number can be obtained through the integrated area of peaks, while the neighbor coordination distance of atoms and the uncertainty of atomic position can be provided by the peak position and the width of the peaks, respectively. It is worth paying special attention on X-ray absorption spectroscopy (XAS) based on synchrotron radiation X-ray, which visualizes the coordination configurations and electronic structures around the target atom [35]. Nuclear magnetic resonance (NMR) can manifest the local symmetry of the specific atoms resulted from the sensitivity to the electric field gradient around the quadrupolar nuclei [36]. Infrared and Raman spectroscopy are expected to identify the present moieties and indicate short-range features [37-39]. Moreover, different from other materials like amorphous carbon/oxides or organic polymers/metal ions, aMOFs possess potential pore distribution. Nitrogen sorption measurement, small angle diffraction, and positron lifetime spectra, etc. are able to acquire the local pore information like pore size distribution [40]. Simulation helps to interrogate the promising aMOF models.
Carefully looking into atomistic models of aMOFs, we can attribute the origin of amorphization mainly to variable local coordination geometry, and disordered arrangements over long range, as shown in Fig.3 [4l]. In view of short range, on one hand, secondary building units (SBUs) contain metal ions or metal clusters. Different valence states or compositions within SBUs would vary the local coordination geometry of SBUs. It is common-sense that the overall structures maintain the basic connectivity and porosity [42-44],but SBUs with distinct shortrange order fail to generate the long-range order seen in cMOF. On the other hand, the distortion/collapse or substitution or vacancy of organic ligands endow random arrangements of both SBUs and linked ligands, resulting in breaking long-range order. Severe distortion of organic ligands would endow pore cavity collapse into a dense amorphous phase and violently break the periodic porosity. Slight distortion together with unequal bonding lengths and angles would also change the distribution of extended framework topology. Such structural changes are always seen in aMOF via top-down approaches. Bottom-up approaches often provide the possibility of substituting organic ligands, including competed ligands and vacant ligands, etc. Such changes would create some distorted or open sites with little long-range order. Regarding long range, changes in framework, including topology, dimensionality, incommensuration, or degree of network interpenetration and so on, would fail to form periodicity. In addition, different features in short range and long range are not independent. Various origins may contribute together to amorphization. Upon deep understanding of the origins of amorphization, it is worth noting that the amorphization does not equal to the absence of selective porosity.
The diverse SBU chemistry, framework topology, and spatial dimensions of the porosity allow the promising utility of aMOFs in many fields [45]. And thus aMOFs exhibit distinct superiorities compared with their crystaline counterparts, as well as classic organic, inorganic, and metallic glasses [l0]. The structuralperformance dependences need in-depth studies to seek opportunities not found elsewhere in material space. Cognitions over different ranges are beneficial for perception the nature of aMOF and promotion of possible applications. Despite the fact that electrochemical energy conversion and storage devices vary depending on the fields, all of them include fundamental chemical and/or physical interactions not only at the surface or interface, but also within the bulks [46-48]. Improving the production, transportation, and utilization of the carriers and other species is one of the essential challenges during electrochemical processes [49].
## 2.1 Short-range characteristics
In common sense, aMOFs seem to disorder over long range but possess short-range order. The distinct local atomic arrangement contributes to chemical disorder or structural disorder. The chemical disorder is always found in amorphous materials due to the preferred element connection in SBUs and/or local coordination geometry of SBUs. Such amorphization can afford abundant distorted or unsaturated coordination sites as possible electrochemical active sites via promoting redox reactions or molecular adsorption [50]. Besides, there is also structural disorder exhibiting in short-range order, which indicates different basic building  units  associated  with variable pore confined environment.This characteristic produces the following advantages in electrochemical reactions. (1） Favorable interfacial charge transfer. Suitable pore confined environment via the amorphization gives rise to facilitate close packing and enrich orbital overlaps between the adjacent ligands [51]. Therefore, the improvement of electrochemical environment at the surface or interface can be realized by structural design of framework [22,24, 25]. It is promising to further achieve favorable interfacial charge transfer based on through-space method. (2) Possible guest accommodation. Partial variation of porosity with distorted or open building blocks enable the accommodation of large guest ions or molecules that cannot be accommodated in the crystal counterparts. It maximizes utilization of their internal voids to trap or release reactant and products during the electrochemical process as adjustable molecular sieves. (3） Acceptable volume change. With the help of more open sites, the volume expanding or contracting will buffer during the permeation and diffusion of guest ions or molecules. Large tensile strain can be suffered to minimize volume change of aMOFs during the electrochemical reactions.
## 2.2 Long-range characteristics
Different from anisotropic crystalline counterparts, aMOFs are isotropic in long range. Such characteristic provides isotropic conducting paths for guests and high ionic conductivity, like $\mathrm{H^{+}}$ and $\operatorname{Li}^{+}$ [52,53]. Free grain boundary in aMOFs can also avoid intergranular channels to achieve the steerable ion transport and reduce meaningless electron loss [54, 55]. In addition, the intrinsic mechanical properties decide the promising applications. Both long-range features contribute to a great extent of nanoductility under load [56],which would be anticipated to suffer more stress changes during electrochemical processes.
## 3 Electrochemical applications
## 3.1 Electrochemical water oxidation
Among the energy conversion systems, electrocatalysis such as water oxidation has been regarded as an impressive way to convert electrical energy into chemical energy. Recently, the aMOFs profited from favorable reaction kinetics have been studied extensively for electrochemical water oxidation. Particularly, the changes of coordination situation for aMOFs between the SBUs and organic ligands can generate coordinatively unsaturated sites to accept electrons and thus facilitate the conversion of products. Up to now, Table 1 summarizes the typical examples about detailed catalytic performances of aMOFs for electrochemical water oxidation.
The aMOFs based on the coordinatively unsaturated metal sites for SBUs have also been expected to offer a path to acquire abundant electrochemical active sites as Lewis acid centers. Recently, the understanding of the crystalline-to-amorphous transformations was  presented to  achieve  the deliberate amorphization engineering for polymetallic SBUs [57]. The synthesis of amorphous ${\mathrm{Co}}_{x}{\mathrm{Fe}}_{y}$ -MOFs via controllable adjustment of the ratio of ${\mathrm{Co}}/{\mathrm{Fe}}$ precursors was employed. Notably, the defects formation energies of $\mathrm{aCo}_{x}\mathrm{Fe}_{y}$ -MOFs could be remarkably decreased with the augmentation of the amount of Fe ions, which generated the long-range disorder structure due to the abundant defects. Importantly, the assembled amorphous ${\mathrm{Co}}_{4}{\mathrm{Fe}}_{6}$ -MOF electrode could afford a low overpotential ( $241\mathrm{mV}$ at $10\mathrm{mA{\cdot}c m^{-2}}$ 0 and a small Tafel slope ( $(30.1\mathrm{mV\cdotdec^{-1}})$ . The synthesized $\mathsf{a C o}_{x}\mathrm{Fe}_{y^{-}}$ MOFs originating from the disordered structure enabled to possess the fast charge transfer and abundant active sites to facilitate the oxygen evolution reaction (OER) activity. The selfsupporting conductive substrates can be employed for the load of aMOFs to expose more electrochemical active sites compared with aMOFs. Amorphous bimetallic MOF was prepared via mild solution phase approach at room temperature, and physicaly deposited on the nickel foam (NF) to fabricate the $\mathrm{Fe}_{x}\mathrm{Ni}_{\mathrm{\Omega}_{\mathrm{3}}}$ (BDC$\mathrm{NH}_{2}^{\cdot}$ /NF electrode [58]. The as-synthesized $\mathrm{Fe}_{x}\mathrm{Ni}_{\mathrm{\Omega}_{\mathrm{\lambda}}}$ (BDC$\mathrm{NH}_{2})/\mathrm{NF}$ benefiting by the high specific surface area $(142.2\mathrm{m}^{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ displayed a low overpotential ( $228\mathrm{mV}$ at $10\mathrm{mA{\cdot}c m^{-2}}$ and a Tafel slope $(30.3~\mathrm{mV\cdotdec^{-1}}^{\cdot}$ ）in alkaline medium. It also demonstrated great stability in continuous l,ooo cycles. Considering that the assembled interface between the aMOFs and substrates via the physical deposition is difficult to control precisely, in situ growth on substrate materials may be a better choice to regulate the growth orientation of aMOFs. To fully and firmly contact with substrates, the one-step bottom-up solvothermal method was employed to fabricate amorphous NiFe MOFs containing threedimensional (3D) macroporous structure on the Ni foam as selfsupporting electrodes [59]. The assembled architecture could display a low overpotential(2ll mVat $10\mathrm{\mA}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2})$ ， outperforming the corresponding cNiFe-MOFs. Moreover, the optimized aMOFs exhibited nearly $100\%$ Faraday efficiency and impressive operational stability. Particularly, the two-electrode system containing the aMOF electrocatalysts with the commercial Pt plate could deliver brilliant overall water splitting activity. The amorphous NiFe-MOF originated from unsaturated coordination of the synergy between Ni and Fe offered the abundant active metal sites, which was the principal possibility of the greatly enhanced activity compared to the crystalline counterpart. The unique hierarchical porosity profiting from the 3D macroporous structure could increase electrolyte contact area and improve mass transport. Furthermore, a facile solvothermal strategy to acquire amorphous bimetallic MOFs $\mathrm{(Fe}_{x}\mathrm{Ni}_{y}$ BDC) was proposed via tailoring the coordination reaction between terephthalic acid $\mathrm{(H_{2}B D C)}$ and metal $(\mathrm{Fe}/\mathrm{Ni})$ ions [22]. Notably, it was discovered that the addition of Ni ions could hinder coordination reaction with $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{BDC},$ which led to the long-range disordered structure (Fig. 4(a)). It could contribute to the acceleration of mass transport and the augmentation of active sites. The assembled electrocatalysts in Fig.4(b) could express a small overpotential $(260~\mathrm{\mV}$ at $10\mathrm{\mA{\cdot}c m^{-2}}$ .Moreover, the optimized structure delivered the impressive Tafel slope $(35~\mathrm{mV\cdotdec^{-1}})$ and a decent Faraday efficiency $(99.5\%)$ as electrocatalysts (Fig.4(c)). The $\mathrm{Fe}_{x}\mathrm{Ni}_{y}$ -BDC afforded rich defects in the metal sites as active sites to improve mass transport.
