## 3.2 Supercapacitor
Electrochemical capacitors, commonly termed supercapacitors, have acquired widely attentions as new-type of energy storage equipment considering their durability and great power density. Electric double layer capacitance and pseudocapacitance are dominant for fast energy storage. The former depends on the reversible electrosorption or charge accumulation without surface redox reaction,while the latter is due to the invertible redox reaction at the electrode-electrolyte interface. The aMOF-based supercapacitors  benefiting from  their inherent properties containing the variable pore confined environment and abundant electrochemical active sites have attracted much attention. Typical aMOFs used for supercapacitors are briefly summarized in Table 2.
The aMOFs originating from their suitable pore confined environment can contribute to the acceleration of electrochemical reaction.The scalable synthesis route made of aNipPD nanosheets was reported in Fig.5(a) [23]. Despite the low surface area of $35\mathrm{m^{2}{\cdot}g^{-1}}$ 、aNi-pPD nanosheets were determined to be a high packing density $(1.50\ \mathrm{g}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-3})$ ，which outperforms the most reported MOFs for supercapacitors. Simultaneously, the aNipPD nanosheets give a micropore size ( $1.65\ \mathrm{nm},$ ）and a large intersheet spacing ( $(1.6\ \mathrm{nm})$ ),which can achieve the reversible and rapid cations intercalation reactions. Within a wide potential window (2.85 V), the aNi-pPD anodes displayed the superior gravimetric capacitance of $259~\mathrm{F{\cdotg}^{-1}}$ and areal capacitance of 2.9 $\mathrm{F}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ at $2\mathrm{A}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ (Fig. 5(b)). The assembled anodes still manifested remarkable stability after over l2,0o0 cycles. Most evidence indicated the reversible and rapid cations intercalation based on the ligands of the aNi-pPD was the main contribution of capacitances (Fig.5(c)). The superior performance of the aMOFbased asymmetric supercapacitors outperformed most traditional crystalline inorganic materials.
Considering that the charge is transferred at or close to the surface of the electrode material, unsaturated coordination situation between the SBUs and organic ligands can generate abundant electrochemical active sites. Yang and co-workers systematically discussed the distinction between amorphous and crystalline zirconium-based MOF (aUiO-66 and cUiO-66) [64]. The aUiO-66 was firstly synthesized by a solvothermal method at $90~^{\circ}\mathrm{C},$ which was regarded as a feasible electrode material for supercapacitors. The aUiO-66 providing more active sites in the aqueous electrolyte of 6 M KOH showed the extraordinary charge storage capability $(920~\mathrm{Fcdotg^{-1}})$ at $10\ \mathrm{mV}{\cdot}\mathrm{s}^{-1}$ 。which was observably superior to the cUiO-66 (452 $\mathrm{F}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}.$ . Particularly, it could be observed that the specific capacitance of aUiO-66 had a gradual descent at the first 300 cycles, which ascribed to the metastable structure of aUiO-66. However, the specific capacitance had brilliantstabilityafter300cycleandsurprisinglymaintainedupto6l0F-g for 5,000 cycles. It revealed that aUiO-66, profiting from the disordered structure of the amorphous framework, was more liable to transform into a relatively stable framework than its crystalline counterpart. Additionally, the specific capacitance of aUiO-66 was assigned to the Faradaic processes occurring at the surfaces and within the electrode, whereas that of cUiO-66 was related to the Faradaic reactions occurring exclusively at the surface. Furthermore, aMOFs profiting from the coordination situation of mixed-metal atoms have also been expected to offer a path to increase the exposed active sites. A facile solution-phase method at room temperature was proposed to achieve the largescale and rapid synthesis of aNi/Co-MOFs, showing excellent specific capacitance [66]. The $\mathrm{aNi}_{2}\mathrm{Co}$ -MOF benefiting the high disorder structure among these aMOFs delivered a great specific capacity $(2,149\ \mathrm{mC}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ at $1\ \mathrm{mA}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}.$ . And it still maintained $79.0\%$ of the initial capacitance after 5,o0o cycles at $5\mathrm{\A\cdotg^{-1}}$ Moreover, the hybrid supercapacitors containing the positive electrode of $\mathrm{aNi}_{2}\mathrm{Co}$ -MOF and the negative electrode of activated carbon displayed impressive energy densities (66.1 and 41.3 Wh·kg-) at power densities of 800 and $8{,}000\mathrm{W}{\cdot}\mathrm{kg}^{-1}.$ 。
## 3.3 Secondary ion battery
Battery, as common electrochemical energy storage devices, plays a pivotal role in the practical application of electric vehicles and portable electronic equipment [67]. Considering the requirements for batteries, abundant redox sites for aMOFs can contribute to improving electrochemical properties. In addition, aMOFs based on the variable pore confined environment can provide more intercalation sites. Thus, aMOFs are expected to achieve largescale practical applications as advanced energy storage materials. And typical aMOFs for secondary ion battery are summarized in Table 3.
The aMOFs are expected to provide more redox sites as possible via the change of coordination structure for SBUs (such as disordered distribution of different valence states).Han and coworkers  systematically  investigated the  microstructure and chemical characteristic evolution of amorphous UiO-66- $\mathrm{NH}_{2}@\mathrm{rGO}$ $\left(\mathrm{U}@\mathrm{rGO}\right)$ via modulating the pyrolysis products [68]. The disproportionation of $\mathrm{Zr^{3+}}$ to $\mathrm{Zr^{2+}/Z r^{4+}}$ states offered the plentiful formed suboxidative metal sites to facilitate the conversion of polysulfide as the transfer and anchor mediator sites. The rearrangement of the octahedral and crystalline UiO-66- $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ into an amorphous state was realized while the abundant functional groups (such as $\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ and COO-) and the relatively high surface area $(298.4~\mathrm{m}^{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ ）were retained. The assembled $\mathrm{U@rGO}$ electrodes could express initial discharge capacities $\mathrm{(906.1\mA{\cdot}h{\cdot}g^{-1}})$ ）and maintain in $801.7\ \mathrm{mA}{\cdot}\mathrm{h}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at the current density of $^\mathrm{~1~C~}$ with a low fading rate ( $0.05\%$ per cycle) after 300 cycles, which revealed the significant sulfur utilization and obvious reversibility. And the improved rate performance for derived electrodes was about $619.1\ \mathrm{mA}{\cdot}\mathrm{h}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at $4\mathrm{C}.$ Notably, the diffusion coefficients of Li ions were dramatically increased by 10-60 times. Thus, the aMOFs as a sulfur host could be explored to alleviate the migration of polysulfide and guarantee fast ions transfer, which offered a convenient channel to activate the metal sites of MOFs.
MOF glass as a sub-class of aMOFs can be melted at an elevated temp prior to decomposition and then quenched to reach the glass state. Considering the modulation of coordination environment (such as the covalent bonding within the organic ligands and/or coordinative interaction between SBUs and organic ligands), the intriguing structural and chemical features for burgeoning MOF glass materials supply more ions intercalation sites. The Co-ZIF62 glass (ZIF glass) anodes for LIBs via melt-quenching method were proposed to take advantage of the disordered structure in
Fig.6(a) [25]. The three types of anodes including ZIF crystal (cZIF),mechanically amorphized ZIF (aZIF), and ZIF glass,were discussed in regard to their structural changes and electrochemical performance. The three samples confirmed that the majority of Co existed as ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ 。 which indicated that SBUs were basically unaffected for cZIF, aZIF, and ZIF glass.However, the new groups of $\mathrm{Co-N}$ could be observed for aZIF and ZIF glass, which proved that changes of organic ligands were the main reason of random arrangement in the long range. Thus, it could make the ions intercalation sites more accessible (Fig.6(b)). The as-synthesized ZIF glass derived the superior capacity $(306~\mathrm{\mA{\cdot}h{\cdot}g^{-1}}$ for 1,000 cycles at $2\mathrm{A}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ ) than the cZIF and aZIF (Fig.6(c)). It was worth noting that the increase of the cycling performances for ZIF glass anodes was clearly observed, and the capacity even tripled after l,o0o cycles. The origin of the continuously rising Li-ion storage capacity with charge-discharge cycling for the ZIF glass could be regarded as the local breakage and abundant distortion of the Co-N coordination bonds between SBUs and organic ligands.
## 4 Bottlenecks and perspectives electrochemical behaviors
The stability of cMOFs has been the theme of abundant research and debate over the past two decades, such as structural and electrochemical stability [37,69]. To date, the phenomenon that the absence of the long-range ordered structure after the first electrochemical process has been already observed in many literatures [70, 71]. Meanwhile, the clarification of the role of the aMOFs in electrochemical process is necessary, considering their impressive performance. Additionally, it is worth noting that amorphous materials belong to the thermodynamically metastable state. The transformation from the amorphous states to their crystalline counterparts often requires to overcome an energy barrier, and thus the amorphous state is not necessarily unstable [72]. However, the disordered structure of aMOFs may provoke more uncertain changes during electrochemical reaction as previously mentioned. More recently, many articles and even reviews claim that their reported aMOFs manifest excellent thermal and chemical stabilities. However, the structural stabilities (in pristine form or at electrolytes) do not guarantee their electrochemical stability [73]. Considerable attention has been paid to the material characterization and performance evaluation of aMOFs so far. These are definitely important, lamentedly, the structural evolution of aMOF in the electrochemical processes is rarely mentioned. Several points for aMOFs during electrochemical reaction are seriously considered below (Fig.7):
(1） Local electrochemical environment.The charge accumulation and local pH changes in the surface of the aMOFs may be destructive with a voltage bias applied. Due to the charge accumulation in an electrochemical environment, the charged surface of the aMOF can attract ions and solvent molecules to form the electrical double layer (EDL). Subsequently, decomposition products of electrolytes and solvent molecules in organic electrolyte solutions can gradually accumulate on the surface of the electrodes. Moreover, the concentration of species such as $\mathrm{H^{+}}$ and $\mathrm{OH^{-}}$ for aqueous electrolytes in the region of the EDL is dramatically different from the pristine electrolyte. As is known to all the majority of MOFs are unstable in acid or base conditions. It may also be one reason for the poor cycling performance of most MOFs [74]. Thus, the evolution of chemical environments at the surface of aMOFs should receive the attention they deserve compared to their pristine form.
(2) Redox reactions. The influence of metal valence changes on the coordination environments of the SBUs cannot be underestimated. The redox reactions at metal sites can break and/or form chemical bonds, indicating the change of frame structure due to the loss of ligands or formation of new coordinate structures.
(3）Thermal issues. It is worthwhile to find out the thermal energy generated during the electrochemical reactions for the safety concern. Thermal energy, such as reversible heat $(Q_{\mathrm{r}})$ polarization heat $(Q_{\mathrm{p}})$ 。and side reaction heat $(Q_{s})$ generates on account of reversible and irreversible electrochemical reactions [75]. Intensive attention is necessary for thermal management on electrode deactivation, electrical imbalance, and capacity/power duraibility, etc.
Based on the above analysis mentioned, understanding the true role of aMOFs during electrochemical process is of paramount importance, considering their possible structural changes of aMOFs under these circumstances [76,77]. One possibility is that no obvious structural variation of aMOFs is observed,which means that aMOFs are the actual active phases. Beyond that, aMOFs may merely be the precursors that provoke active ingredient during the electrochemical reactions. On the basis of the extent of metal-ligand bonding, there are two possibilities below.
(1） Existence of the metal-ligand bonding. Under the circumstances, no significant damage to the basic framework of aMOFs may be discovered. With an increasing/decreasing potential applied, the variation in disorder may occur for the aMOFs at the first several cycles. Due to the structural rearrangement based on the role of the electric field, the pristine aMOFs may tend to change into a more stable amorphous structure.
(2） Loss of the metal-ligand bonding. Dramatic structural changes may make aMOFs as sacrificial templates to derive various nanomaterials such as amorphous metallic compounds (metal oxides or metal hydroxides) and/or amorphous carbon materials.
To date, a small but growing number of electrochemical analysis of aMOFs has started to appear. Regrettably, most studies attributed the reactive sites simply to metal sites and ligands like their crystalline counterparts. Among them, postanalysis is a typical method via comparing the morphologic and structural changes before/after reactions. However, the conclusion of postanalysis limited by the possible contamination of samples after reactions may not be entirely reliable. The instant potential-driven structural variation of aMOFs makes it paramount significant to analyse holistically the different stages of electrochemical process via adopting various  techniques to gather  evidence [78]. Simultaneously, the intrinsic complexity of amorphous structures for aMOFs in the targeted reactions makes it comparatively difficult to study the various electrochemical stage. Generally, in situ techniques benefiting by reproducing of the electrochemical environment are better choices than postanalyses, including the in situ physical characterization method and in situ electrochemical characterization method [79-81].
In situ physical characterization method focusing on structural variation has been regarded as essential measures to yield observations of the structural evolution. As illustrated in Fig.7, the different spatial scales containing atomic scale, mesoscale, and macroscale can be categorized to analyse the instant evolutionary process of electrodes. In situ techniques based on X-ray analyses as the well-established techniques are fundamental to provide structural information of electrodes at the atomic scale [82]. Though the loss of the long-range structure for aMOFs induces the absence of obvious Bragg diffraction, X-ray diffraction (XRD) is still significative [83]. It can determine whether the crystalamorphous transition during electrochemical reactions has occurred. XAS analysis as the synchrotron-associated technique depends on the X-ray absorption for selected elements of materials. XAS analysis is classified as hard XAS (hXAS) and soft XAS (sXAS) on account of the energy range of the synchrotron Xrays. The sXAS reflects structural and chemical information, such as symmetry, orbital occupancy, and oxidation state. The hXAS detects local atomic structures, such as the type of neighboring atoms, coordination number, and bond distance. However, the beam damage on electrodes under X-ray irradiation is still unavoidable [84]. The sampling-time optimization study, such as their flux, specific beam energy, and exposure time, should be completed to minimize beam damage and noise. The data reliability can be guaranteed via the stable baseline measurement in open-circuit conditions. Moreover, spectroscopy based on the interaction between electromagnetic waves and materials is also an important tool, including in situ Fourier transform infrared spectroscopy (FT-IR) and Raman spectroscopy analysis [85,86]. The in situ FT-IR analysis based on the absorption of molecular vibrations can recognize the presence of various organic moieties. However, the solvent molecules of the electrolytes and their decomposition products may preclude the determination of structural measurement of aMOFs, especially organic electrolytes. The in situ Raman spectroscopy analysis based on the Raman scattering as an effective tool has received the attention it deserves, which can contribute to the identification of short-range ordering considering the disordered nature of aMOFs. The damage to the amorphous structure deserves attention, considering the energy exchange between photons and the molecules during the inelastic collision process. Thus, it is crucial to select the incident light source. Transmission electron microscopy (TEM) at the atomic scale and mesoscale can record localized structural information due to the strong interactions between electrons and materials [87]. In situ TEM analysis profiting from the vigorous ability to capture transient structural variation is an ideal tool for electrodes to explore morphological evolution and phase transitions during electrochemical process such as volume changes of electrodes along with reactions. Notably, electron beam irradiation can induce amorphous structures to crystal phases considering the radiation-sensitive feature. More recently, cryogenic conditions and low beam dose environment with the development of TEM instrumentation have been achieved to obtain reliable information, which contributes to the investigation of aMOFs [88]. Furthermore, it is worth noting that macroscale change of electrodes is equally universal. The in situ strain evolution analysis can get insights into the irreversible (such as solid-electrolyte interface) and relative reversible (such as phase transformations) deformations upon ions intercalation into electrodes [89]. The change of electrode thickness can be observed via the in situ dilatometry  analysis to investigate the volume changes experienced upon electrochemical cycling [90].
Though the in situ physical characterization methods are widely used,it is recently that the in situ electrochemical characterization methods have also been regarded as essential measures considering the intrinsic complexity of non-crystalline structures. Particularly, the in situ electrochemical characterization methods concerning the change of carriers can afford intuitionistic evidence of the charge transfer and ion migration. Thus, the in situ electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) analysis profiting from the lower dependence on crystallinity makes it possible to determine more details and establish a relatively accurate relationship between electrochemical stages and the potential range of electrodes [91, 92]. It is found that the characterization of structural change limited from lack of phase transition via physical characterization methods is diffcult to recognize the various stages of the electrochemical reaction process. However, the change in charge transfer impedance can be observed via using in situ EIS analysis to record the charge-discharge process [93]. Moreover, the ion migration is the basis for electrode design. The in situ/operando electron energy-loss spectroscopy (EELS) analysis via real-time tracking of ions migration to distinguish kinetic pathways provides direct means. Zhang and co-workers proposed an ionic liquid electrolyte-based cell for the Li-EELS with high time resolution [94]. Due to the high sensitivity of lithium ions at the local environment, their coordination environment and electronic structure could be shed light directly, revealing the possible ion-occupied sites.
In brief, the basic possibilities and future directions of aMOFs for further development of the structural evolution during electrochemical process are detailed in energy conversion and storage. Importantly, the in situ techniques of structural evolution during electrochemical reaction can offer the essential understanding to found a structure-mechanics-performance relationship for aMOFs. Simultaneously, considering the complicated amorphous structure and the limitations of the single technique, complementary probing analyses combining different techniques can conduce to the complete cognition of the role of aMOFs toward practical applications.
Based on understanding the key feature of aMOFs during electrochemical process, controlled synthesis and structural modulation are of paramount importance. Top-down approaches are achieved via introducing external driving forces to break the long-range periodicity of cMOFs [14]. Considering the difficulty in accurately controlling the external forces, bottom-up approaches can be ideal route to achieve structural modulation of aMOFs. Ligand competition method via introducing other ligands during the synthesis is most commonly route. As an example, ligands like l,4-benzenedicarboxylic acid and isophthalic acid were used to directly synthesize amorphous spherical MOFs [20]. Besides, interfering with the coordination process between metal ions and ligands is also proposed via optimization of reaction parameters like reaction temperature and variation of the modulator quantity to disrupt long-range periodicity [64, 95]. For instance, aUiO-66 can be prepared directly from synthesis by varying the reaction temperature [64]. Therefore, it is promising to reinforce cognition of aMOF domains with MOF versatility and shed light on the structural modulation for efficient electrochemical energy applications via amorphization.
